  <h1>India’s Agriculture and Climate Change — Building Resilience for the Future</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Agriculture supports nearly 45% of India’s workforce and remains the backbone of rural livelihoods. Yet, as The Hindu highlighted in 2024, agriculture is one of the sectors most vulnerable to climate change. Erratic monsoons, droughts, and rising temperatures threaten food security and farmer incomes. For UPSC, this theme bridges environment, economy, and social justice.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Share in GDP (2024): ~18%.<br/>•	Rainfed Dependency: ~52% of cultivated area depends on monsoon rains.<br/>•	Climate Losses: ICAR estimates 4–9% decline in agri GDP by 2030 due to climate change.<br/>•	Crop Yields: Heat stress reducing wheat productivity; floods hitting paddy.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Challenges<br/>1.	Rainfall Variability: Monsoon delays, uneven distribution.<br/>2.	Water Stress: Groundwater depletion in Punjab, Haryana, Gujarat.<br/>3.	Soil Degradation: Erosion, salinity, chemical overuse.<br/>4.	Pest/Disease Outbreaks: Climate change altering pest cycles.<br/>5.	Farmer Vulnerability: Smallholders most exposed to risks.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Government Measures<br/>•	National Mission on Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA): Soil health, rainfed farming.<br/>•	Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchai Yojana (PMKSY): Irrigation and water efficiency.<br/>•	PM Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY): Insurance coverage for crop losses.<br/>•	Soil Health Card Scheme: Promoting balanced fertiliser use.<br/>•	National Innovations on Climate Resilient Agriculture (NICRA): R&D on climate-resilient crops.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Opportunities<br/>1.	Millets Revolution: Nutri-cereals more climate-resilient.<br/>2.	Digital Agriculture: AI, satellite-based advisories.<br/>3.	Agroforestry & Diversification: Enhancing soil, water, income security.<br/>4.	Green Finance: Climate funds, carbon credits for farmers.<br/>5.	Community Models: Watershed management, farmer collectives.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Food Security: Stable crop production vital for 1.4 billion people.<br/>•	Farmer Welfare: Protects incomes, reduces agrarian distress.<br/>•	Economic Growth: Agriculture sustains rural demand.<br/>•	Global Commitments: Paris Agreement goals linked to sustainable agriculture.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-III (Agri & Environment): Food security, sustainability.<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Welfare schemes, farmer policies.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Climate change is the biggest risk multiplier for Indian agriculture.”<br/>o	“Resilient agriculture is key to sustainable growth.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Resource politics and development.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Water-Smart Agriculture: Micro-irrigation, aquifer recharge.<br/>2.	Diversify Cropping: Shift from paddy-wheat to millets, pulses, oilseeds.<br/>3.	Strengthen Insurance: Universal, affordable crop insurance.<br/>4.	Technology Integration: Drones, AI-based pest/disease alerts.<br/>5.	Farmer Education: Climate awareness and adaptive practices.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Agriculture’s resilience will determine India’s food security, farmer welfare, and sustainability goals. Policies must combine technology, financial support, and ecological approaches. For UPSC aspirants, it illustrates how climate change, economy, and governance converge in the rural development agenda.</p>
